course: everyday
lesson: 197
title: Lies that do real harm
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some lies cause real damage to other people or to yourself. Blaming someone innocent, accusing without proof, or getting someone into trouble on purpose are among the worst, because an innocent person suffers.
explain: Other lies are dangerous because the people who keep you safe need the truth: doctors and nurses, parents who need to know where you are, and emergency services. Hiding a bad report or a medicine you swallowed only stops adults from helping.
explain: Example: in the old story «The Boy Who Cried Wolf», a shepherd boy shouts «Wolf!» for fun again and again. When a real wolf comes, nobody believes him. False alarms make it harder to be believed when it truly matters.
explain: If you have told a harmful lie, the bravest and best thing is to tell the truth quickly, before the harm grows.
fact: Blaming someone else for what you did hurts them twice: they get punished and they lose trust in you.
fact: Never accuse someone of something you have not seen and cannot prove; a false accusation can damage a person's name for a long time.
fact: Lying to get someone else in trouble is a serious wrong, and if you did it, telling the truth quickly is the way to fix it.
fact: False alarms make it harder for you to be believed when you really need help.
fact: Doctors and nurses need true facts to treat you properly, and they are there to help, not to punish.
fact: Parents and teachers can only help with problems they know about.
fact: Your parents or carers need to know where you are and who you are with, so they can find you if something goes wrong.
fact: Age limits on apps and games, often 13, exist to protect children from content and contacts that are not safe.
fact: Never joke about swallowing pills or being hurt; if it is true, tell an adult at once.
fact: Never prank call an emergency number like 911, 999, 112, 000 or 111; it ties up help someone really needs and is against the law.
exercise: You broke a mug and are tempted to say your sister did it. Why is that so harmful? | She gets punished unfairly and loses trust in you | It is fine if she has broken things before, because then it evens out | It only matters if the mug was expensive | Sisters always forgive each other quickly
exercise: Someone says a classmate stole money, but nobody saw it. What should you do? | Not repeat an accusation that nobody can prove | Tell everyone in your class in case it turns out true | Search the classmate's bag yourself when nobody looks | Stop speaking to the classmate at once, just in case it is true
exercise: A child tells the teacher a lie to get another child in trouble. What is right if you did this? | Tell the truth quickly so the innocent child is not punished | Keep quiet, because it is too late now and the trouble will pass | Tell another lie to make it smaller | Wait to see if anyone finds out
exercise: What is the lesson of «The Boy Who Cried Wolf»? | False alarms make it hard to be believed when help is really needed | Wolves only come when someone shouts | Shepherds should never look after sheep | It is fine to shout for fun as long as nobody is near enough to hear it
exercise: You got hurt doing something you were told not to do, and a doctor asks how it happened. What should you do? | Tell the truth so the doctor can treat you properly | Make up a story so you do not get told off by your mum and dad | Say you cannot remember anything at all | Let your friend answer for you instead
exercise: You got a bad school report. What is wise? | Show it to your parents and talk about it | Hide it in a drawer until the holidays are over | Sign it yourself and hand it back so nobody at home has to worry | Throw it in a bin on the way home from school
exercise: Why must you tell your parents the truth about where you go after school? | If something goes wrong they need to know where to find you | Because parents like to know every little thing their children do all day | So they can tell your friends' parents | It only matters if you go somewhere far away
exercise: A friend lies about his age to join an app for over-13s. Why do the age limits exist? | To protect children from content and contacts that are not safe | Because younger children cannot read the small writing on the screen yet | So that apps can charge older children more | Because the apps only work for adults
exercise: A boy says he swallowed some pills «as a joke». What is right? | Never joke about it, and if it is true tell an adult at once | Laugh along, because he said it was a joke | Wait to see if he feels ill tomorrow | Tell him to drink lots of water and keep quiet so no one gets worried
exercise: A child phones 999 for fun and hangs up. Why is this so wrong? | It ties up help that someone in a real emergency needs | The phone bill will be very expensive | The operator will be bored by the call | It is only wrong if you say a rude word to the person who answers
exercise: Which lie hurts an innocent person the most? | Saying a classmate cheated when you know she did not | Saying you liked a meal you found too salty | Saying «nothing» when your friend asks about her own surprise party | Telling a story about a talking cat
exercise: A child keeps pretending to be hurt to get attention. What problem does this cause? | People may not believe her when she is really hurt | Everyone will look after her even more closely than before | It teaches the others to be kinder and more careful with her | It makes her real injuries heal much more quickly later
test: A girl in Calgary told the teacher her friend pushed her, but it was not true, and now the friend has lost recess. What should she do now? | Go to the teacher and tell the truth straight away | Say nothing, since recess is only short | Give the friend a sweet to make up for it and say nothing to the teacher | Tell another story to make it less bad
test: A boy in Brisbane hurt his arm jumping off a shed and tells the nurse he slipped. Why is this a bad idea? | The nurse needs the real story to check the right injuries | Nurses tell your parents every word you say, so it gets you in trouble | Slipping injuries are always worse | It is only a bad idea if the arm is broken
test: Why might people stop believing someone who often raises false alarms? | They learn that his alarms are usually not real | Because they cannot hear him properly from far away | Because false alarms are always much too quiet for people to notice | Because people do not like loud children who shout
test: A girl says she is going to a friend's house but goes to the park instead. What is the danger? | If something goes wrong nobody knows where to look | Her friend will be upset that she did not come | The park is closed to children in the afternoon | Her parents will never trust the friend again
test: Which is the best reason never to blame your brother for your mess? | He would be punished for something he did not do | Because he might blame you for his own mess next time instead | Because it is hard to remember who did what | Because parents can always tell who did it
test: A boy in Manchester hears that a girl «stole» a phone, but no one saw anything. What should he do with this story? | Not pass it on, since it is unproven and could be false | Tell everyone, so they can keep their phones safe | Ask the girl to show him what is in her bag | Believe it, because so many people are saying it
test: Why should a child not hide a bad mark from her parents? | They can only help with problems they know about | They will never ask about your school marks anyway | Hiding it makes the mark go up by itself next time | Teachers will change the mark later if no one at home ever sees it
test: In New Zealand, the emergency number is 111. When should a child call it? | Only in a real emergency, never as a prank | Whenever she is bored at home alone | To test whether the phone is working properly before a real emergency | To ask what time the shops open
test: spilt juice on the sofa, gonna say it was my sister | No, blaming her hurts her twice; own up | Fine, she spills stuff all the time anyway, so they'll believe it | Fine if she doesn't find out | Fine if you say it quietly
test: my mate wants to prank call 999 lol | No, it takes help from people who need it, and it's against the law | Fine if you hang up fast | Fine if it's for a joke | Fine if you say your real name and tell them it's only a joke at the end
test: im 10, app needs u to be 13, just put a fake birthday | No, age limits are there to keep children safe | Yes, everyone does it | Yes, birthdays don't matter online because nobody ever checks them | Yes, if your friend has it
test: doc asked if i ate anything weird, i did but i dont wanna say | Tell the truth, the doctor needs real facts to help | Say no, what you eat is private and the doctor doesn't need it | Make up something else | Say you can't remember
explain: A boy in Ohio lied that a classmate had stolen his money; the classmate was punished for weeks before the truth came out, and the boy lost his friends' trust.
explain: A girl in Leeds spread a made-up story about a friend online; the friend was so upset she would not come to school, and the girl had to face the head teacher and her parents.
fact: Harmful lies can get innocent people punished and badly hurt their feelings.
fact: Spreading untrue stories about someone online can reach many people and can get you into serious trouble.
test: gonna say tom took my pen so i don't get told off | Don't, he'd be punished for nothing, tell the truth | Say it, it saves you | Blame the teacher | Blame everyone
test: someone made up a rumour about my mate online | Don't share it, support your friend and tell an adult | Share it with a few people to find out whether it's true | Add more to the story | Laugh and forget it
test: why's a harmful lie worse than a white lie | It hurts or gets someone in trouble | It's not worse, both are fine | White lies are always worse | Harmful lies are just jokes
test: did u hear Dan rang 999 for a laugh n the police came to his school n took him away in handcuffs!! | Never prank call 999, it ties up real help; the handcuffs may be blown up | Prank calls are fine if you hang up fast before anyone can trace the call | Police never mind prank calls | 999 is only for adults
test: everyone's saying Josh stole Ella's money, Mia said so, nobody saw it tho, share it? | Don't share it; accusing without proof can badly hurt an innocent person | Share it, Mia must know | Add that he stole more | Punish Josh yourself first, before the teachers even get to find out about it
test: apparently Finn swallowed some of his mum's pills for a dare n said nothing, they say his stomach exploded | Tell an adult at once if anyone swallows pills; the exploding bit is a rumour | Wait and see if he feels ill | Never tell anyone, because he'll get into big trouble with his mum for taking them | Swallowing pills is safe for kids
exam: Identify the principal reason why false alarms are harmful, as illustrated by "The Boy Who Cried Wolf". | They make it harder to be believed when help is truly needed | They make wolves come to the village more often than before | They help people practise for real dangers in the future | They are harmless as long as they are said only for fun
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of telling doctors and nurses the truth? | They need true facts to treat one properly | They wish to report one to the head teacher | They punish all the children who are not honest | They need the facts only for their own records
exam: It is given that a person has falsely blamed another for a wrong. Ascertain the means of putting it right. | Tell the truth quickly, before the harm grows | Wait until the other person forgets about it | Apologise privately but let the blame stand | Say nothing, since the lie is already told
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning age limits on apps and games. | They exist to protect children from unsafe content and contacts | They exist only so that companies can sell more to adults | They are suggestions that children may safely ignore | They apply only to games played on computers at school
